Guided Exercise: Kubernetes and OpenShift CLI
Goal of the Exercise
This lab teaches you how to:
1.	Access an OpenShift cluster using CLI
2.	Use authentication token
3.	Compare kubectl vs oc
4.	Identify cluster components and resources
________________________________________
1. Preparing the Lab Environment
Command:
lab start cli-interfaces
What it does
This command prepares the lab environment.
It:
•	creates required cluster resources
•	configures the workstation
•	ensures the cluster is ready
So you can safely run the exercise.
________________________________________
2. Access the OpenShift Web Console
Open browser:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
Login with:
Username: developer
Password: developer
Purpose
The OpenShift Web Console is the GUI interface used to:
•	manage cluster
•	deploy applications
•	download CLI tools
•	get login tokens
________________________________________
3. Locate the oc CLI Tool
Inside the console:
Help (?) → Command Line Tools
This page provides downloads for:
•	oc CLI
•	kubectl CLI
For operating systems like:
•	Linux
•	Windows
•	Mac
•	ARM
Important concept:
oc package also includes kubectl
So installing oc automatically gives kubectl.
In this lab, CLI is already installed, so no download needed.
________________________________________
4. Get Login Token
Next step is authentication.
Open:
Command Line Tools → Copy login command
Login again with developer account.
Click:
Display Token
You will see something like:
oc login --token=sha256-xxxx \
--server=https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
________________________________________
5. Login to Cluster Using Token
Command:
oc login --token=sha256-xxxxx \
--server=https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
What happens
This command authenticates the user with the cluster API server.
Components involved:
User → oc CLI → API Server → OAuth Authentication
Output example:
Logged into "https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443" as "developer"
Meaning:
•	authentication successful
•	CLI now connected to cluster
________________________________________
6. Compare kubectl Commands
Command:
kubectl help
Purpose
Lists all available kubectl commands.
Example commands:
Command	Purpose
create	create resource
expose	expose service
run	run container
set	change object configuration
get	list resources
delete	delete resources
explain	show documentation
Example output:
kubectl controls the Kubernetes cluster manager
________________________________________
Important Observation
kubectl does NOT have login command.
Because Kubernetes normally uses:
kubeconfig authentication
But OpenShift adds its own login system.
________________________________________
7. Explore kubectl create Command
Command:
kubectl create --help
Purpose
Shows:
•	command syntax
•	examples
•	subcommands
•	options
Example usage:
kubectl create -f pod.yaml
Meaning:
Create resource from YAML file.
________________________________________
Available subcommands
Examples:
clusterrole
clusterrolebinding
configmap
cronjob
deployment
These represent Kubernetes resources.
________________________________________
Important Option
--dry-run
Example:
kubectl create deployment nginx --dry-run=client
Meaning:
Do not actually create resource.
Just show what would happen.
________________________________________
8. Compare oc Commands
Command:
oc help
This lists OpenShift CLI commands.
Example output:
Basic Commands:
login
new-project
new-app
status
project
projects
explain
________________________________________
Important Difference
kubectl	oc
Kubernetes CLI	OpenShift CLI
No login command	Has login command
No project creation	Can create projects
No application creation	Has new-app
________________________________________
Example OpenShift Commands
Login
oc login
Authenticate user.
________________________________________
Create project
oc new-project myproject
Creates new namespace/project.
________________________________________
Create application
oc new-app
Deploys app from:
•	source code
•	container image
________________________________________
9. Compare oc create Command
Command:
oc create --help
It includes:
•	all kubectl create features
•	additional OpenShift resources
Example OpenShift resources:
build
imagestream
route
clusterresourcequota
